      <h1>USCIS Case Outside Normal Processing Time: What Actually Moves a Stuck Case</h1>
      <p class="post-byline">Reviewed by <a href="/meet-our-team/">Maisoun Sulfab, Attorney at Law</a> &middot; Published November 3, 2026</p>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Your case is late when your receipt date is past USCIS's posted case inquiry date. Then you escalate in order: e-Request, DHS Form 7001 to the CIS Ombudsman, a congressional inquiry, and finally a mandamus suit under 28 U.S.C. § 1361. Mandamus compels a decision, not an approval.</p>
<p>Most people jump straight to the lawsuit. That's a mistake, and an expensive one. The clients who get a decision fastest can prove on paper that the wait is costing them something specific: a job with a start date, an EAD running out, a child about to age out. A San Francisco <a href="/immigration-lawyer-san-francisco/">immigration lawyer</a> can usually tell you which rung you're on in fifteen minutes.</p>

