<h2 id="why-stuck">Why Your Case Sits for Months</h2>
<p>USCIS doesn't run one queue. It runs dozens, and your case is in exactly one of them. That's why two people who filed the same form on the same day can be 14 months apart.</p>
<ul>
<li>A security check returns "pending" with no date attached. Nobody can tell you when it clears, because the answer isn't in the system.</li>
<li>The file moves between a service center, the National Benefits Center, and a local office. The clock effectively restarts at each stop, so you're measuring against the wrong office.</li>
<li>An RFE cycle. You respond in 30 days, then the case joins the back of a queue. Two rounds can add a year.</li>
<li>No visa number. For an I-485 in a preference category, the Visa Bulletin controls, and no lawsuit moves your priority date.</li>
</ul>
<p>"My case is stuck" usually translates to "my case is in a queue I didn't know existed." Find out which queue first.</p>

<h2 id="case-inquiry-date">Find Your Real Case Inquiry Date First</h2>
<p>Two official tools do the work, and both are free.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Case status.</strong> Put your receipt number into <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/case-status">uscis.gov/case-status</a>. "Case was received" tells you nothing about timing. "Case is at a local office" tells you the file moved.</li>
<li><strong>Processing times.</strong> Open the <a href="https://egov.uscis.gov/processing-times/">USCIS processing times tool</a>, pick your form, category, and office, then read the "When can I ask about my case?" line. That's your case inquiry date, often later than the number quoted on forums.</li>
</ol>
<p>Posted times are averages, not deadlines. USCIS says so itself. Past the posted date you've earned the right to ask a question, not the right to sue.</p>
<p>What makes a suit viable is a different statute. Under 5 U.S.C. § 555(b), "within a reasonable time, each agency shall proceed to conclude a matter presented to it." Congress added a number in 8 U.S.C. § 1571(b): the sense of Congress that a benefit application should be completed within 180 days of filing, and within 30 days for a nonimmigrant visa petition under § 1184(c). Courts treat it as a signal, not a stopwatch.</p>

